Will Ghislaine Maxwell's Testimony Be Televised? No. The deposition will be held in closed session and conducted virtually, meaning the public will not be able to watch it live. Comer has said Maxwell is likely to invoke her right against self-incrimination. Rep. Robert Garcia (D-Calif.), the committee’s ranking member, said Maxwell is receiving special treatment and criticized what he described as a continuing cover-up. "}], "web_search_calls": 1, "web_searches_remaining": 2, "belief": {"p": 0.15, "base_rate_anchor": "", "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Ghislaine Maxwell was convicted in December 2021 and is serving a 20-year sentence", "Her prison sentence limits her ability to travel or testify publicly", "No known congressional subpoenas or invitations to testify have been publicly announced"], "key_uncertainties": ["Whether Congress will issue a subpoena", "Whether her sentence could be reduced to allow testimony", "Whether Congress will prioritize this testimony"], "confidence": "low", "searches_tried": ["Ghislaine Maxwell congressional testimony Congress Epstein subpoena"], "step": 1, "update_reasoning": "Starting from a low base rate - Maxwell is currently imprisoned for Epstein-related crimes. No evidence yet of congressional testimony arrangement."}, "hint": "Call lookup_url on a promising result URL to read the full page."}

